  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Access to Clean Water, Sanitation and Hygiene</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">For over four years, the province of Cabo Delgado in Mozambique has experienced brutal attacks by armed groups, while neighbouring provinces of Nampula, Niassa, Zambezia and Sofala are seeing a spillover of displacement. As a result of the insecurity, 946,508 people have been displaced in Northern Mozambique as of June 2022 and the humanitarian situation continues to deteriorate in both hosting and displaced communities, and for those left behind. Provision of sanitation services next to water supply is one of the most important needs among the internally displaced persons (IDP) sites to reduce the practice of open defecation and the prevention of water-borne diseases, especially cholera outbreaks, which has already affected several IDP sites in 2021 due to the lack of latrines and proper maintenance. Through this project, IOM contributes to ensuring dignified, safe, and secure living conditions and basic services for IDPs in Cabo Delgado. In particular, through this intervention, IOM is facilitating the construction of sustainable and durable infrastructures for water supply and sanitation designed to be appropriate and scalable for future upgrades and inclusive for people with disabilities.</narrative>
  </description>
